### Checked other resources

- [x] I added a very descriptive title to this issue.
- [x] I searched the LangGraph.js documentation with the integrated search.
- [x] I used the GitHub search to find a similar question and didn't find it.
- [x] I am sure that this is a bug in LangGraph.js rather than my code.
- [x] The bug is not resolved by updating to the latest stable version of LangGraph (or the specific integration package).

### Example Code

```js
import { Annotation, END, MemorySaver, START, StateGraph } from "@langchain/langgraph";

const State = Annotation.Root({ x: Annotation(), rounds: Annotation() });
const Child = Annotation.Root({ x: Annotation() });

function program(checkpointer) {
  const child = new StateGraph(Child)
    .addNode("increment", (state) => ({ x: state.x + 1 }))
    .addEdge(START, "increment").addEdge("increment", END)
    .compile({ checkpointer });
  return new StateGraph(State)
    .addNode("child", child)
    .addNode("tick", (state) => ({ rounds: state.rounds + 1 }))
    .addEdge(START, "child").addEdge("child", "tick")
    .addConditionalEdges("tick", (state) => (state.rounds < 2 ? "child" : END), ["child", END])
    .compile({ checkpointer: new MemorySaver() });
}

async function run(checkpointer, interruptBefore) {
  const graph = program(checkpointer), config = { configurable: { thread_id: "1" }, interruptBefore };
  let result = await graph.invoke({ x: 0, rounds: 0 }, config);
  for (let i = 0; i < 5 && (await graph.getState(config)).next.length; i++)
    result = await graph.invoke(null, config);  // Continue past the breakpoint.
  return result.x;
}

for (const checkpointer of [undefined, true])
  for (const interruptBefore of [undefined, ["child"]])
    console.log(`checkpointer=${checkpointer}, interruptBefore=${JSON.stringify(interruptBefore)}: x =`,
                await run(checkpointer, interruptBefore));

if ((await run(true, ["child"])) !== 2) throw new Error("the second subgraph call ignored its input");
```

### Error Message and Stack Trace (if applicable)

```text
checkpointer=undefined, interruptBefore=undefined: x = 2
checkpointer=undefined, interruptBefore=["child"]: x = 2
checkpointer=true, interruptBefore=undefined: x = 2
checkpointer=true, interruptBefore=["child"]: x = 1
file:///.../issue.mjs:32
if ((await run(true, ["child"])) !== 2) throw new Error("the second subgraph call ignored its input");
                                              ^

Error: the second subgraph call ignored its input
```

### Description

A parent graph calls the same subgraph twice in one thread, from a loop. The subgraph is compiled with `checkpointer: true`, so it keeps its own state per thread. The run pauses at a static breakpoint (`interruptBefore: ["child"]`) and is continued with `invoke(null, config)`.

I expect the second call to run the subgraph on its new input, giving `x = 2`. That is what happens without the breakpoint, and without `checkpointer: true`. Instead, the second call does not run the subgraph at all: it returns the first call's result, and `x = 1`. There is no error or warning.

It is worse when the subgraph's state shares a key that the parent's loop advances. If `Child` also declares `rounds`, the stale result writes `rounds: 0` back every time, so the loop never ends: after any number of continuations the thread is still at `x = 1, rounds = 1` and paused before `child`. The same happens with `interruptAfter: ["tick"]`, with `interruptBefore: "*"`, and with the breakpoints given to `compile()` instead of the call.

The cause is `isResuming` in `pregel/loop`:

```ts
const configIsResuming = configHasResumingFlag && this.config.configurable?.[CONFIG_KEY_RESUMING];
...
return hasChannelVersions && (configIsResuming || inputIsNullOrUndefined || ...);
```

When the parent continues past the breakpoint, it sets `CONFIG_KEY_RESUMING` in the config of the whole run, and the subgraph task inherits it. The subgraph's thread already holds the finished checkpoint of its first call, so `hasChannelVersions` is true, and the new call is treated as a resumption of that finished run. Its input is never applied. A subgraph without `checkpointer: true` gets a fresh checkpoint namespace per task, so it has no channel versions and starts on its input.

Python LangGraph has the same behaviour, reported in langchain-ai/langgraph#9346.


### System Info

- `@langchain/langgraph` 1.6.0 (latest), `@langchain/langgraph-checkpoint` 1.2.2, `@langchain/core` 1.3.2; `main` at 2f7b082 has the same check
- Node v22.23.3, macOS

